{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824955144,"sessionID":"ses_f461ef367ffe0p6nB1IHi4bUUU","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e172510016Xt0hWaIUGNT0X","sessionID":"ses_f461ef367ffe0p6nB1IHi4bUUU","messageID":"msg_0b9e168f6001uyCWnY1qc006cJ","type":"tool","id":"call_00_aLB20KgLzH0gyUCLGTiz5202","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":260,"offset":1468,"path":"/tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt, lines 1468-1727\n1468: Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini and Sheff Halsey, our chief marketing officer and an executive vice president, and representatives from Goodwin Procter were\n1469: also present for some or all of the meeting. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Board its fiduciary duties in the context of considering strategic alternatives\n1470: that might be available to Mac-Gray, including a potential sale of the company. The Board also discussed, with input from the members of the Transaction Committee, whether it would be appropriate for\n1471: each of Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg to be excluded from the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives in view of the possibility that Mr. MacDonald and/or Moab could be\n1472: asked to partner with a potential acquiror given their respective equity stakes in Mac-Gray. Following this discussion, the Board decided, and Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg agreed,\n1473: that it would be appropriate to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives and any negotiations with third parties that might take\n1474: place. Mr. MacDonald excused himself after such discussion. In furtherance of the conclusion to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic\n1475: alternatives, the Board then established the Special Committee, comprised of the six independent and disinterested members of the Board (namely, Mr. Bullock, Mr. Daoust,\n1476: Mr. Hyman, William F. Meagher, Bruce A. Percelay and Alastair G. Robinson), to, among other things, explore strategic alternatives to enhance value to Mac-Gray's stockholders and to recommend\n1477: to the Board the advisability of any such strategic alternative. In connection with this delegation of authority, the Special Committee was further authorized to (a) consider and evaluate all\n1478: proposals that might be received by Mac-Gray in connection with a possible sale or other significant business transaction, through any form of transaction, including, without limitation, merger, stock\n1479: purchase, asset purchase, recapitalization, reorganization, going-private transaction, consolidation, amalgamation or other transaction, (b) participate in and direct the negotiation of the\n1480: material terms and conditions of any such transaction, (c) consider any alternatives to any such transaction, including without limitation, Mac-Gray's continuing to operate as an independent\n1481: company and (d) recommend to the Board the advisability of entering into a definitive agreement with respect to any such transaction. Mr. Bullock was appointed chairman of the Special\n1482: Committee. It was also determined that the Transaction Committee would function as a sub-committee of the Special Committee for the purpose of taking any administrative actions to discharge the duties\n1483: and responsibilities of the Special Committee.\n1484: 30\n1485: Table of Contents\n1486: Mr. Rothenberg\n1487: was then excused and the Board meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma\n1488: and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were present for all or portions of the meeting. Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma presented Mac-Gray's five-year strategic business\n1489: plan and projections, including the with acquisitions case and the without acquisitions case (see \"—Projected Financial Information\" below), which we refer to as the Projections. Following\n1490: the management presentation, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch discussed potential strategic alternatives and the structure and timing of a potential sale process. The Special Committee reviewed\n1491: Mac-Gray's short and long-term business strategies and discussed the five-year strategic business plan, including Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities\n1492: management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of\n1493: general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price.\n1494: The Special Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a\n1495: significant or transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring.\n1496: The\n1497: Special Committee, in follow up to the considerations introduced at the May 9\n1498: th\n1499: Board meeting, then further considered whether it might be advisable to\n1500: approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with third parties, including Party A, at this time. The Special Committee discussed the potential risks and benefits of commencing a process in which\n1501: parties would be invited to review confidential information and submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray, focusing again on the potential\n1502: disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might arise from approaching potential acquirors within the industry and the resulting impact on Mac-Gray's business and the need to disclose\n1503: during such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential competitors. The members of the Special Committee also discussed with BofA Merrill Lynch the\n1504: likelihood that strategic parties, including CSC and Party A, should be able to pay a higher price for Mac-Gray because of the benefit of synergies, and noted their collective belief that Party A\n1505: would likely increase the Party A June 21 proposal in light of such synergies if Party A were given the opportunity to conduct business and legal due diligence on a potential acquisition of\n1506: Mac-Gray. The Special Committee then discussed the other strategic alternatives, including a significant share repurchase program and an extraordinary dividend, and determined that these options were\n1507: not likely to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders as compared to execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business plan or undertaking a sale of the company. In light of the factors discussed at this\n1508: meeting, the Special Committee concluded that it would be in the best interests of stockholders to consider a potential sale of Mac-Gray and to review this alternative as compared to Mac-Gray's\n1509: continuing as a stand-alone company and the risks and opportunities associated with execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business plan.\n1510: The\n1511: Special Committee, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch and management, also discussed the general and specific universe of potential acquirors who might be contacted, including\n1512: those parties the Special Committee believed had the strongest potential to have an interest in engaging in a strategic transaction with Mac-Gray. In its evaluation of potential likely acquirors, the\n1513: Special Committee considered such parties' financial strength, their experience in acquiring companies in Mac-Gray's industry, such parties' perceived potential interest in Mac-Gray and potential\n1514: synergies based on such parties' businesses and legal considerations. The Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch also discussed that although potential strategic buyers should be able to pay a\n1515: higher purchase price because of synergies, it was important to solicit interest from financial buyers, including private equity firms, to maximize the competitive dynamics in the sale process. This\n1516: perspective was based on the fact that many financial buyers had engaged in transactions with companies in industries similar to Mac-Gray and could be in a position to recognize value competitive with\n1517: potential strategic buyers. Guided by the foregoing principles, the Special Committee determined to approach a total of\n1518: 31\n1519: Table of Contents\n1520: 50 parties,\n1521: including CSC and Pamplona, who together we refer to as CSC/Pamplona, and Party A, to solicit potential interest in a transaction with Mac-Gray, 15 of which were potential strategic\n1522: buyers and 35 of which were potential financial buyers, including private equity firms.\n1523: During\n1524: the next several weeks, in accordance with the directives of the Special Committee, BofA Merrill Lynch contacted the strategic and financial parties identified at the\n1525: June 24th Special Committee meeting. Over the next two months a total of 20 potential bidders, including two strategic bidders (Party A and CSC/Pamplona) and 18 financial bidders\n1526: (including Party B and Party C), entered into confidentiality agreements with Mac-Gray on a form provided by Mac-Gray, the terms of which were individually negotiated with potential\n1527: bidders, but did not vary materially from the form provided. The confidentiality agreements contained customary non-disclosure provisions and a customary standstill provision that either expired or\n1528: permitted the potential bidder to make confidential proposals to Mac-Gray (a so-called \"sunset\" provision) upon announcement of a business combination between Mac-Gray and a third party, and, in some\n1529: instances upon announcement of a change in control. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package for the purpose of\n1530: providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest, which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and\n1531: standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest, which they were\n1532: instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. The letter requested that the proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, material conditions, including\n1533: required approvals, and focus areas for due diligence.\n1534: On\n1535: June 28, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter. BofA Merrill\n1536: Lynch summarized the contacts they had had with certain of the potential bidders. In particular, BofA Merrill Lynch reported that they had spoken with representatives of Party A, as instructed by the\n1537: Special Committee, who had expressed the view that there would be considerable synergies if Party A were to acquire Mac-Gray and that Party A would be speaking with certain financing sources with\n1538: respect to funding such a transaction. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported that they had spoken with representatives of Pamplona, as instructed by the Special Committee, who expressed interest in a\n1539: transaction, including an acquisition of Mac-Gray by its portfolio company, CSC. Pamplona emphasized that business and legal due diligence would be minimal given its familiarity with Mac-Gray and the\n1540: industry generally.\n1541: Later\n1542: on June 28, 2013, Party B, entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing\n1543: Party B with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1544: On\n1545: June 30, 2013, Party C entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party C\n1546: with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1547: On\n1548: July 3, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg called a representative of Goodwin Procter to indicate that he had spoken with representatives of Pamplona about Moab's desire to maintain\n1549: the flexibility to consider a possible equity rollover if Pamplona were to participate in a business combination with Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg also informed Goodwin Procter that Pamplona would\n1550: be requesting that any confidentiality agreement CSC/Pamplona execute with Mac-Gray permit CSC and Pamplona to discuss their bid and evaluation material with Moab, with Moab agreeing to exclusively\n1551: engage with CSC/Pamplona.\n1552: On\n1553: July 6, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of receiving an update on the ongoing sale process. By invitation of the Special Committee,\n1554: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present throughout the meeting. The Special Committee discussed whether Moab should be permitted to partner exclusively with\n1555: CSC/Pamplona. Because the\n1556: 32\n1557: Table of Contents\n1558: objective\n1559: of the sale process was to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee decided to deny the exclusivity request at this stage of the process so that the potential equity\n1560: rollover of Moab's shares could be available as a source of capital for other potential bidders. The Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter to communicate to CSC/Pamplona\n1561: and Mr. Rothenberg, respectively, that it was premature for Mac-Gray to permit Moab to engage in equity rollover discussions with any party, including CSC/Pamplona, but that such discussions\n1562: would be permitted at a later stage of the sale process.\n1563: On\n1564: July 11, 2013, CSC/Pamplona entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and were provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing\n1565: CSC/Pamplona with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1566: Between\n1567: July 11, 2013 and July 23, 2013, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona engaged in a preliminary review of Mac-Gray and the potential acquisition opportunity. Also\n1568: during this time, outside counsel to Party A and representatives of Goodwin Procter negotiated the terms of a confidentiality and standstill agreement, but did not come to agreement on the terms of\n1569: the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions.\n1570: On\n1571: July 23, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $18.50 per share.\n1572: On\n1573: July 24, 2013, Party B submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $17.00 to $18.00 per share. Also on July 24, 2013, representatives\n1574: from Party C called BofA Merrill Lynch and presented an oral preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $15.00 to $17.00 per share.\n1575: On\n1576: July 25, 2013, the Board held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. Shea and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin\n1577: Procter were also present for all or portions of this meeting. Prior to the start of the meeting, because the Board expected to discuss matters related to the sale process, and in light of Moab's\n1578: continuing interest in a potential equity rollover in any sale transaction, Mr. Rothenberg recused himself from the meeting and the meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. At\n1579: this meeting, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed the preliminary indications of interest received from Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona as well as the Party A June 21 proposal, in\n1580: each case from a financial point of view. The Board reviewed management's five-year strategic business plan and Projections. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the\n1581: Special Committee its fiduciary duties in the context of considering a potential sale. The Special Committee then discussed the potential conflict of interest arising from Moab's continuing desire to\n1582: consider an equity rollover and decided that Mr. Rothenberg should be asked to recuse himself from participating in Board meetings for the duration of the sale process so he would not be privy\n1583: to confidential information about Mac-Gray and our business.\n1584: The\n1585: Special Committee discussed that the preliminary indications of interest were lower than expected and suggested that additional due diligence, including with regard to the potential\n1586: synergies, should result in improved offers by the strategic parties. The Special Committee then discussed the highly competitive nature of Mac-Gray's industry and expressed concern over permitting\n1587: access to and the potential misuse of sensitive and competitive information, including customer terms and locations, especially by strategic bidders. Accordingly, the Special Committee concluded that\n1588: it would be advisable to stage the disclosure of such information to each of the bidders to the extent that a bidder's indication of interest and other actions demonstrated its seriousness in\n1589: acquiring Mac-Gray on terms viewed favorably by the Special Committee. Based on this discussion, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed with the Special Committee a proposed process for the second stage of the\n1590: on-going sale process. As part of this review, BofA Merrill Lynch recommended that management meetings be arranged between members of Mac-Gray management (other than Mr. MacDonald) and each of\n1591: Party A (if Party A\n1592: 33\n1593: Table of Contents\n1594: agreed\n1595: to enter into a confidentiality agreement), Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to provide these parties with access to high level information concerning Mac-Gray's technology, pipeline,\n1596: management team, regional operations, contracts, operations and growth initiatives, and in the case of CSC/Pamplona and Party A, synergies, and to then request that each of these parties submit a\n1597: revised indication of interest. After discussion and in an effort to drive offers with increased value, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to proceed with the staged\n1598: disclosure as discussed at this meeting and to reach out to each of the four interested bidders and arrange management meetings.\n1599: Later\n1600: on July 25, 2013, Party C submitted a written indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $16.50 per share.\n1601: Discussions\n1602: with Party A resumed on July 27, 2103 and Party A and the Transaction Committee agreed to the terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions. On\n1603: August 5, 2013, Party A entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party A with data to\n1604: potentially improve the terms of the Party A June 21 proposal.\n1605: On\n1606: August 6, 2013, representatives of Party B attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1607: BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the Transaction Committee.\n1608: On\n1609: August 8, 2013, representatives of Party C attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1610: BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the Transaction Committee.\n1611: On\n1612: August 12, 2013, representatives of CSC and Pamplona attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management,\n1613: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members of the Transaction Committee.\n1614: On\n1615: August 14, 2013, representatives of Party A attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1616: BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members of the Transaction Committee.\n1617: On\n1618: August 15, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of reviewing the on-going strategic alternatives process. By invitation of the Special\n1619: Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also in attendance. BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the current status of the strategic alternatives\n1620: process, including the due diligence process and the management presentations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. After discussion and as discussed at the\n1621: July 25\n1622: th\n1623: Special Committee meeting, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to send a letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and\n1624: CSC/Pamplona once all four of the bidders' due diligence was substantially complete requesting that, in light of the results of the respective party's due diligence, such party submit a revised\n1625: written proposal in order to continue in the process, including to engage in more in-depth and confirmatory due diligence of Mac-Gray.\n1626: Between\n1627: August 6, 2013 and August 27, 2013, the four bidders conducted business and legal due diligence. During this time, Party B and Party C, in light of the fact that\n1628: they were financial bidders, were given broad access to an electronic data site, and Party A and CSC/Pamplona, in light of the fact that they were strategic bidders, were given somewhat limited access\n1629: to this electronic data site, with access to customer data, including customer locations, being reserved until later in the process as part of the staging discussed at the\n1630: July 25\n1631: th\n1632: Special Committee meeting. During this time, both Party A and CSC/Pamplona participated in discussions with members of Mac-Gray management around synergies.\n1633: 34\n1634: Table of Contents\n1635: On\n1636: August 27, 2013, BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Special Committee, sent a letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona instructing such bidders to\n1637: submit revised written proposals no later than September 9, 2013 if such bidders wished to proceed further in the process to pursue an in-depth evaluation of Mac-Gray with the objective of\n1638: making a revised offer. The letter requested that the revised proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, regulatory approvals and other post-signing\n1639: covenants and focus areas for due diligence.\n1640: Also\n1641: on August 27, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party B participated in a telephone conference call for\n1642: Party B to conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1643: On\n1644: August 29, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party C participated in an in-person session for Party C to\n1645: conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1646: On\n1647: September 3, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party A participated in a telephone conference call for Party\n1648: A to conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1649: On\n1650: September 9, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $19.50 per share, indicating that Pamplona was committed to\n1651: provide 100% of the capital required to consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to exclusive negotiations for two weeks. On September 9, 2013, Party B\n1652: also submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.50 per share and proposed to finance the transaction with a combination of equity from affiliated funds and third\n1653: party debt. Party B's revised indication of interest did not include a firm financing commitment.\n1654: On\n1655: September 10, 2013, Party A submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share, to be financed by outstanding credit\n1656: facilities or affiliated equity sources. Also on September 10, 2013, in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch, a representative of Party C indicated that it was submitting\n1657: a revised oral indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $17.00 per share. Neither Party A's nor Party C's revised indication of interest included a firm financing\n1658: commitment.\n1659: On\n1660: September 11, 2013, members of the Transaction Committee, in a telephone call, without identifying Party A, Party B, Party C or CSC/Pamplona and without indicating any proposed\n1661: purchase prices, provided Mr. MacDonald with a limited overview of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives, including the decision to engage in strategic\n1662: alternatives process, and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC/Pamplona so that Mr. MacDonald could consider whether he continued to have an interest in potentially rolling over any of his\n1663: Mac-Gray shares in a transaction. During this call, Mr. MacDonald indicated that he had decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in any transaction.\n1664: Later\n1665: on September 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to review the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona.\n1666: By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were present for all or portions of the meeting.\n1667: BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee the current status of the revised indications of interest. The Special Committee also reviewed and discussed the Projections, including the risks\n1668: and opportunities relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company. The Special Committee also considered the Projections in light of the revised\n1669: indications of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch then indicated that, based on conversations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, it was unlikely that any of these parties would move forward in\n1670: the process without the express support of Mr. MacDonald. The Transaction Committee reported that Mr. MacDonald had\n1671: 35\n1672: Table of Contents\n1673: decided\n1674: that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in a transaction and recommended that Mr. MacDonald be fully briefed of the process to date. The Special Committee agreed,\n1675: noting that Mr. MacDonald's perspective on the purchase price of a sale transaction was critical to the Special Committee's evaluation, as any potential acquiror would be unlikely to proceed\n1676: without knowing that Mr. MacDonald would support the transaction. Based on the amount of due diligence conducted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, and discussions with such parties\n1677: to date, and in an effort to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to call each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to request final\n1678: indications of interest by September 18, 2013 and to indicate that Mac-Gray would consider engaging in a short period of exclusive negotiations with one of the bidders depending upon, among\n1679: other things, the proposed purchase price and the level of deal certainty indicated in the bidder's final proposal. As authorized and instructed by the Special Committee, representatives of BofA\n1680: Merrill Lynch called each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona on September 11, 2013 with such instructions.\n1681: On\n1682: September 12, 2013, at the instruction of the Transaction Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. MacDonald\n1683: and provided him with a detailed overview of the strategic alternatives process, including the status of negotiations with and the identity of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. Although\n1684: Mr. MacDonald voiced his general support for continuing with the process, he expressed his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for stockholders.\n1685: Between\n1686: September 11, 2013 and September 18, 2013, Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona continued to conduct business and legal due diligence.\n1687: On\n1688: September 18, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a proposal for an all-cash purchase price of $20.75 per share, reiterating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital\n1689: required to consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to engage in exclusive negotiations for two weeks. Also on September 18, 2013, Party A reiterated in a\n1690: telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch that its previous indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share was its best and final offer. On\n1691: September 18, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with a purchase price that Party B valued at $21.50 per share, including $19.00 of cash to be paid at closing\n1692: and the remaining per share price to be paid in the form of options to be issued to Mac-Gray stockholders, representing 10% of the new equity with a strike price equal to Party B's initial cost basis.\n1693: These options, which we refer to as the Party B options, would vest on the achievement of the base case management projections (\n1694: i.e.\n1695: , over five years),\n1696: excluding any future acquisitions. Party B's proposal stated that it valued the Party B options at $2.50 of incremental value per share. Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or\n1697: reiterate its prior indication of interest of an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $17.00 per share nor did it specify any reasons in connection therewith.\n1698: On\n1699: September 19, 2013, the Special Committee held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Shea, as well as\n1700: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the current\n1701: status of the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B and CSC/Pamplona and reported that Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or reiterate its prior indication\n1702: of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported to the Special Committee on Mr. MacDonald's general support for continuing with the process and his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a\n1703: higher value for stockholders. Mr. Shea again reviewed the Projections with the Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch. The Special Committee again discussed the Projections, including the\n1704: risks relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry\n1705: facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller\n1706: 36\n1707: Table of Contents\n1708: local\n1709: and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price). The Special\n1710: Committee also again discussed the potential opportunities if Mac-Gray were able to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including the ability to improve profitability\n1711: and EBITDA margins by increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from\n1712: coin-operated technology to card-based technology). The Special Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with\n1713: increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring. The Special Committee then considered the Projections in light\n1714: of the revised indications of interest. Representatives from Goodwin Procter again reviewed the Special Committee's fiduciary duties in the context of a sale of the company. The Special Committee then\n1715: discussed each of the three revised proposals, including their advantages and disadvantages. The Special Committee discussed the risks and complexities of the Party B options, including their\n1716: contingent and deferred nature and the uncertainties associated with how Mac-Gray's performance would be measured, especially in light of the fact that current Mac-Gray management would not be in\n1717: control of the surviving corporation to monitor or drive the growth necessary to trigger payment under the Party B options.\n1718: The\n1719: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch were then excused from the meeting and the Special Committee continued its discussion. The Special Committee noted that the indication of\n1720: interest submitted by Party B, including the Party B options, was on its face higher than the CSC/Pamplona proposal but further discussed the risks and complexities involved with measuring and\n1721: achieving the performance required to vest the Party B options. The Special Committee also noted that Party B would likely be financing the transaction with a combination of equity from affiliated\n1722: funds and third party debt capital, which would involve more risk than the Pamplona-funded CSC/Pamplona proposal, which did not require any third party financing. Following a discussion, the Special\n1723: Committee concluded that the revised indication of interest submitted by CSC/Pamplona had the potential of delivering the most value to stockholders, provided greater certainty and involved less\n1724: potential risk than the other indications. However, the Special Committee also concluded that, primarily in view of potential synergies between Mac-Gray and CSC, CSC/Pamplona should be in a position\n1725: to increase their offer price above $20.75. The Special Committee then authorized the Transaction Committee to inform CSC/Pamplona that the Special Committee would be prepared to move into exclusivity\n1726: at a price of $21.25 per share and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to negotiate the terms of such exclusivity based on guidance provided by the Special Committee. In addition,\n1727: at this meeting Mr. MacDonald indicated that he would be supportive of a price of $21.25 per share.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1728]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt, lines 1468-1727\n1468: Mr. Shea, Mr. MacLellan, Mr. Emma, Ms. Serafini and Sheff Halsey, our chief marketing officer and an executive vice president, and representatives from Goodwin Procter were\n1469: also present for some or all of the meeting. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the Board its fiduciary duties in the context of considering strategic alternatives\n1470: that might be available to Mac-Gray, including a potential sale of the company. The Board also discussed, with input from the members of the Transaction Committee, whether it would be appropriate for\n1471: each of Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg to be excluded from the Board's consideration of strategic alternatives in view of the possibility that Mr. MacDonald and/or Moab could be\n1472: asked to partner with a potential acquiror given their respective equity stakes in Mac-Gray. Following this discussion, the Board decided, and Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg agreed,\n1473: that it would be appropriate to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic alternatives and any negotiations with third parties that might take\n1474: place. Mr. MacDonald excused himself after such discussion. In furtherance of the conclusion to exclude Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg from the Board's evaluation of strategic\n1475: alternatives, the Board then established the Special Committee, comprised of the six independent and disinterested members of the Board (namely, Mr. Bullock, Mr. Daoust,\n1476: Mr. Hyman, William F. Meagher, Bruce A. Percelay and Alastair G. Robinson), to, among other things, explore strategic alternatives to enhance value to Mac-Gray's stockholders and to recommend\n1477: to the Board the advisability of any such strategic alternative. In connection with this delegation of authority, the Special Committee was further authorized to (a) consider and evaluate all\n1478: proposals that might be received by Mac-Gray in connection with a possible sale or other significant business transaction, through any form of transaction, including, without limitation, merger, stock\n1479: purchase, asset purchase, recapitalization, reorganization, going-private transaction, consolidation, amalgamation or other transaction, (b) participate in and direct the negotiation of the\n1480: material terms and conditions of any such transaction, (c) consider any alternatives to any such transaction, including without limitation, Mac-Gray's continuing to operate as an independent\n1481: company and (d) recommend to the Board the advisability of entering into a definitive agreement with respect to any such transaction. Mr. Bullock was appointed chairman of the Special\n1482: Committee. It was also determined that the Transaction Committee would function as a sub-committee of the Special Committee for the purpose of taking any administrative actions to discharge the duties\n1483: and responsibilities of the Special Committee.\n1484: 30\n1485: Table of Contents\n1486: Mr. Rothenberg\n1487: was then excused and the Board meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma\n1488: and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were present for all or portions of the meeting. Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma presented Mac-Gray's five-year strategic business\n1489: plan and projections, including the with acquisitions case and the without acquisitions case (see \"—Projected Financial Information\" below), which we refer to as the Projections. Following\n1490: the management presentation, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch discussed potential strategic alternatives and the structure and timing of a potential sale process. The Special Committee reviewed\n1491: Mac-Gray's short and long-term business strategies and discussed the five-year strategic business plan, including Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities\n1492: management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of\n1493: general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price.\n1494: The Special Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with increasing the Company's stock price without a\n1495: significant or transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring.\n1496: The\n1497: Special Committee, in follow up to the considerations introduced at the May 9\n1498: th\n1499: Board meeting, then further considered whether it might be advisable to\n1500: approach and discuss a potential sale transaction with third parties, including Party A, at this time. The Special Committee discussed the potential risks and benefits of commencing a process in which\n1501: parties would be invited to review confidential information and submit indications of interest with respect to a potential business combination involving Mac-Gray, focusing again on the potential\n1502: disruptions to Mac-Gray's business, the risk of leaks that might arise from approaching potential acquirors within the industry and the resulting impact on Mac-Gray's business and the need to disclose\n1503: during such process sensitive, proprietary and confidential information to competitors and potential competitors. The members of the Special Committee also discussed with BofA Merrill Lynch the\n1504: likelihood that strategic parties, including CSC and Party A, should be able to pay a higher price for Mac-Gray because of the benefit of synergies, and noted their collective belief that Party A\n1505: would likely increase the Party A June 21 proposal in light of such synergies if Party A were given the opportunity to conduct business and legal due diligence on a potential acquisition of\n1506: Mac-Gray. The Special Committee then discussed the other strategic alternatives, including a significant share repurchase program and an extraordinary dividend, and determined that these options were\n1507: not likely to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders as compared to execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business plan or undertaking a sale of the company. In light of the factors discussed at this\n1508: meeting, the Special Committee concluded that it would be in the best interests of stockholders to consider a potential sale of Mac-Gray and to review this alternative as compared to Mac-Gray's\n1509: continuing as a stand-alone company and the risks and opportunities associated with execution of Mac-Gray's strategic business plan.\n1510: The\n1511: Special Committee, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch and management, also discussed the general and specific universe of potential acquirors who might be contacted, including\n1512: those parties the Special Committee believed had the strongest potential to have an interest in engaging in a strategic transaction with Mac-Gray. In its evaluation of potential likely acquirors, the\n1513: Special Committee considered such parties' financial strength, their experience in acquiring companies in Mac-Gray's industry, such parties' perceived potential interest in Mac-Gray and potential\n1514: synergies based on such parties' businesses and legal considerations. The Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch also discussed that although potential strategic buyers should be able to pay a\n1515: higher purchase price because of synergies, it was important to solicit interest from financial buyers, including private equity firms, to maximize the competitive dynamics in the sale process. This\n1516: perspective was based on the fact that many financial buyers had engaged in transactions with companies in industries similar to Mac-Gray and could be in a position to recognize value competitive with\n1517: potential strategic buyers. Guided by the foregoing principles, the Special Committee determined to approach a total of\n1518: 31\n1519: Table of Contents\n1520: 50 parties,\n1521: including CSC and Pamplona, who together we refer to as CSC/Pamplona, and Party A, to solicit potential interest in a transaction with Mac-Gray, 15 of which were potential strategic\n1522: buyers and 35 of which were potential financial buyers, including private equity firms.\n1523: During\n1524: the next several weeks, in accordance with the directives of the Special Committee, BofA Merrill Lynch contacted the strategic and financial parties identified at the\n1525: June 24th Special Committee meeting. Over the next two months a total of 20 potential bidders, including two strategic bidders (Party A and CSC/Pamplona) and 18 financial bidders\n1526: (including Party B and Party C), entered into confidentiality agreements with Mac-Gray on a form provided by Mac-Gray, the terms of which were individually negotiated with potential\n1527: bidders, but did not vary materially from the form provided. The confidentiality agreements contained customary non-disclosure provisions and a customary standstill provision that either expired or\n1528: permitted the potential bidder to make confidential proposals to Mac-Gray (a so-called \"sunset\" provision) upon announcement of a business combination between Mac-Gray and a third party, and, in some\n1529: instances upon announcement of a change in control. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package for the purpose of\n1530: providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest, which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. Parties that had entered into confidentiality and\n1531: standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest, which they were\n1532: instructed to submit by July 23, 2013. The letter requested that the proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, material conditions, including\n1533: required approvals, and focus areas for due diligence.\n1534: On\n1535: June 28, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a telephone conference call with representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter. BofA Merrill\n1536: Lynch summarized the contacts they had had with certain of the potential bidders. In particular, BofA Merrill Lynch reported that they had spoken with representatives of Party A, as instructed by the\n1537: Special Committee, who had expressed the view that there would be considerable synergies if Party A were to acquire Mac-Gray and that Party A would be speaking with certain financing sources with\n1538: respect to funding such a transaction. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported that they had spoken with representatives of Pamplona, as instructed by the Special Committee, who expressed interest in a\n1539: transaction, including an acquisition of Mac-Gray by its portfolio company, CSC. Pamplona emphasized that business and legal due diligence would be minimal given its familiarity with Mac-Gray and the\n1540: industry generally.\n1541: Later\n1542: on June 28, 2013, Party B, entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing\n1543: Party B with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1544: On\n1545: June 30, 2013, Party C entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party C\n1546: with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1547: On\n1548: July 3, 2013, Mr. Rothenberg called a representative of Goodwin Procter to indicate that he had spoken with representatives of Pamplona about Moab's desire to maintain\n1549: the flexibility to consider a possible equity rollover if Pamplona were to participate in a business combination with Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg also informed Goodwin Procter that Pamplona would\n1550: be requesting that any confidentiality agreement CSC/Pamplona execute with Mac-Gray permit CSC and Pamplona to discuss their bid and evaluation material with Moab, with Moab agreeing to exclusively\n1551: engage with CSC/Pamplona.\n1552: On\n1553: July 6, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of receiving an update on the ongoing sale process. By invitation of the Special Committee,\n1554: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present throughout the meeting. The Special Committee discussed whether Moab should be permitted to partner exclusively with\n1555: CSC/Pamplona. Because the\n1556: 32\n1557: Table of Contents\n1558: objective\n1559: of the sale process was to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee decided to deny the exclusivity request at this stage of the process so that the potential equity\n1560: rollover of Moab's shares could be available as a source of capital for other potential bidders. The Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter to communicate to CSC/Pamplona\n1561: and Mr. Rothenberg, respectively, that it was premature for Mac-Gray to permit Moab to engage in equity rollover discussions with any party, including CSC/Pamplona, but that such discussions\n1562: would be permitted at a later stage of the sale process.\n1563: On\n1564: July 11, 2013, CSC/Pamplona entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and were provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing\n1565: CSC/Pamplona with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.\n1566: Between\n1567: July 11, 2013 and July 23, 2013, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona engaged in a preliminary review of Mac-Gray and the potential acquisition opportunity. Also\n1568: during this time, outside counsel to Party A and representatives of Goodwin Procter negotiated the terms of a confidentiality and standstill agreement, but did not come to agreement on the terms of\n1569: the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions.\n1570: On\n1571: July 23, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $18.50 per share.\n1572: On\n1573: July 24, 2013, Party B submitted a preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $17.00 to $18.00 per share. Also on July 24, 2013, representatives\n1574: from Party C called BofA Merrill Lynch and presented an oral preliminary indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $15.00 to $17.00 per share.\n1575: On\n1576: July 25, 2013, the Board held a regularly scheduled in-person meeting. By invitation of the Board, Mr. Shea and representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin\n1577: Procter were also present for all or portions of this meeting. Prior to the start of the meeting, because the Board expected to discuss matters related to the sale process, and in light of Moab's\n1578: continuing interest in a potential equity rollover in any sale transaction, Mr. Rothenberg recused himself from the meeting and the meeting proceeded as a meeting of the Special Committee. At\n1579: this meeting, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed the preliminary indications of interest received from Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona as well as the Party A June 21 proposal, in\n1580: each case from a financial point of view. The Board reviewed management's five-year strategic business plan and Projections. During this meeting, representatives of Goodwin Procter reviewed for the\n1581: Special Committee its fiduciary duties in the context of considering a potential sale. The Special Committee then discussed the potential conflict of interest arising from Moab's continuing desire to\n1582: consider an equity rollover and decided that Mr. Rothenberg should be asked to recuse himself from participating in Board meetings for the duration of the sale process so he would not be privy\n1583: to confidential information about Mac-Gray and our business.\n1584: The\n1585: Special Committee discussed that the preliminary indications of interest were lower than expected and suggested that additional due diligence, including with regard to the potential\n1586: synergies, should result in improved offers by the strategic parties. The Special Committee then discussed the highly competitive nature of Mac-Gray's industry and expressed concern over permitting\n1587: access to and the potential misuse of sensitive and competitive information, including customer terms and locations, especially by strategic bidders. Accordingly, the Special Committee concluded that\n1588: it would be advisable to stage the disclosure of such information to each of the bidders to the extent that a bidder's indication of interest and other actions demonstrated its seriousness in\n1589: acquiring Mac-Gray on terms viewed favorably by the Special Committee. Based on this discussion, BofA Merrill Lynch discussed with the Special Committee a proposed process for the second stage of the\n1590: on-going sale process. As part of this review, BofA Merrill Lynch recommended that management meetings be arranged between members of Mac-Gray management (other than Mr. MacDonald) and each of\n1591: Party A (if Party A\n1592: 33\n1593: Table of Contents\n1594: agreed\n1595: to enter into a confidentiality agreement), Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to provide these parties with access to high level information concerning Mac-Gray's technology, pipeline,\n1596: management team, regional operations, contracts, operations and growth initiatives, and in the case of CSC/Pamplona and Party A, synergies, and to then request that each of these parties submit a\n1597: revised indication of interest. After discussion and in an effort to drive offers with increased value, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to proceed with the staged\n1598: disclosure as discussed at this meeting and to reach out to each of the four interested bidders and arrange management meetings.\n1599: Later\n1600: on July 25, 2013, Party C submitted a written indication of interest at an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $16.50 per share.\n1601: Discussions\n1602: with Party A resumed on July 27, 2103 and Party A and the Transaction Committee agreed to the terms of the customer and employee non-solicitation provisions. On\n1603: August 5, 2013, Party A entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party A with data to\n1604: potentially improve the terms of the Party A June 21 proposal.\n1605: On\n1606: August 6, 2013, representatives of Party B attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1607: BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the Transaction Committee.\n1608: On\n1609: August 8, 2013, representatives of Party C attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1610: BofA Merrill Lynch and a member of the Transaction Committee.\n1611: On\n1612: August 12, 2013, representatives of CSC and Pamplona attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management,\n1613: representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members of the Transaction Committee.\n1614: On\n1615: August 14, 2013, representatives of Party A attended a management presentation at the Boston office of Goodwin Procter with members of Mac-Gray management, representatives of\n1616: BofA Merrill Lynch and the two members of the Transaction Committee.\n1617: On\n1618: August 15, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting for the purpose of reviewing the on-going strategic alternatives process. By invitation of the Special\n1619: Committee, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also in attendance. BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the current status of the strategic alternatives\n1620: process, including the due diligence process and the management presentations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. After discussion and as discussed at the\n1621: July 25\n1622: th\n1623: Special Committee meeting, the Special Committee authorized and instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to send a letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and\n1624: CSC/Pamplona once all four of the bidders' due diligence was substantially complete requesting that, in light of the results of the respective party's due diligence, such party submit a revised\n1625: written proposal in order to continue in the process, including to engage in more in-depth and confirmatory due diligence of Mac-Gray.\n1626: Between\n1627: August 6, 2013 and August 27, 2013, the four bidders conducted business and legal due diligence. During this time, Party B and Party C, in light of the fact that\n1628: they were financial bidders, were given broad access to an electronic data site, and Party A and CSC/Pamplona, in light of the fact that they were strategic bidders, were given somewhat limited access\n1629: to this electronic data site, with access to customer data, including customer locations, being reserved until later in the process as part of the staging discussed at the\n1630: July 25\n1631: th\n1632: Special Committee meeting. During this time, both Party A and CSC/Pamplona participated in discussions with members of Mac-Gray management around synergies.\n1633: 34\n1634: Table of Contents\n1635: On\n1636: August 27, 2013, BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Special Committee, sent a letter to each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona instructing such bidders to\n1637: submit revised written proposals no later than September 9, 2013 if such bidders wished to proceed further in the process to pursue an in-depth evaluation of Mac-Gray with the objective of\n1638: making a revised offer. The letter requested that the revised proposals address, among other things, valuation, sources and structures of financing, regulatory approvals and other post-signing\n1639: covenants and focus areas for due diligence.\n1640: Also\n1641: on August 27, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party B participated in a telephone conference call for\n1642: Party B to conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1643: On\n1644: August 29, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party C participated in an in-person session for Party C to\n1645: conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1646: On\n1647: September 3, 2013, members of Mac-Gray management, representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of Party A participated in a telephone conference call for Party\n1648: A to conduct follow-up and confirmatory due diligence.\n1649: On\n1650: September 9, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $19.50 per share, indicating that Pamplona was committed to\n1651: provide 100% of the capital required to consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to exclusive negotiations for two weeks. On September 9, 2013, Party B\n1652: also submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.50 per share and proposed to finance the transaction with a combination of equity from affiliated funds and third\n1653: party debt. Party B's revised indication of interest did not include a firm financing commitment.\n1654: On\n1655: September 10, 2013, Party A submitted a revised indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share, to be financed by outstanding credit\n1656: facilities or affiliated equity sources. Also on September 10, 2013, in a telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch, a representative of Party C indicated that it was submitting\n1657: a revised oral indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $17.00 per share. Neither Party A's nor Party C's revised indication of interest included a firm financing\n1658: commitment.\n1659: On\n1660: September 11, 2013, members of the Transaction Committee, in a telephone call, without identifying Party A, Party B, Party C or CSC/Pamplona and without indicating any proposed\n1661: purchase prices, provided Mr. MacDonald with a limited overview of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives, including the decision to engage in strategic\n1662: alternatives process, and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC/Pamplona so that Mr. MacDonald could consider whether he continued to have an interest in potentially rolling over any of his\n1663: Mac-Gray shares in a transaction. During this call, Mr. MacDonald indicated that he had decided that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in any transaction.\n1664: Later\n1665: on September 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to review the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona.\n1666: By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Shea and Mr. Emma, as well as representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were present for all or portions of the meeting.\n1667: BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee the current status of the revised indications of interest. The Special Committee also reviewed and discussed the Projections, including the risks\n1668: and opportunities relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company. The Special Committee also considered the Projections in light of the revised\n1669: indications of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch then indicated that, based on conversations with Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, it was unlikely that any of these parties would move forward in\n1670: the process without the express support of Mr. MacDonald. The Transaction Committee reported that Mr. MacDonald had\n1671: 35\n1672: Table of Contents\n1673: decided\n1674: that he was not interested in rolling over any of his shares in a transaction and recommended that Mr. MacDonald be fully briefed of the process to date. The Special Committee agreed,\n1675: noting that Mr. MacDonald's perspective on the purchase price of a sale transaction was critical to the Special Committee's evaluation, as any potential acquiror would be unlikely to proceed\n1676: without knowing that Mr. MacDonald would support the transaction. Based on the amount of due diligence conducted by Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona, and discussions with such parties\n1677: to date, and in an effort to maximize value for Mac-Gray's stockholders, the Special Committee instructed BofA Merrill Lynch to call each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona to request final\n1678: indications of interest by September 18, 2013 and to indicate that Mac-Gray would consider engaging in a short period of exclusive negotiations with one of the bidders depending upon, among\n1679: other things, the proposed purchase price and the level of deal certainty indicated in the bidder's final proposal. As authorized and instructed by the Special Committee, representatives of BofA\n1680: Merrill Lynch called each of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona on September 11, 2013 with such instructions.\n1681: On\n1682: September 12, 2013, at the instruction of the Transaction Committee, representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. MacDonald\n1683: and provided him with a detailed overview of the strategic alternatives process, including the status of negotiations with and the identity of Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona. Although\n1684: Mr. MacDonald voiced his general support for continuing with the process, he expressed his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a higher value for stockholders.\n1685: Between\n1686: September 11, 2013 and September 18, 2013, Party A, Party B, Party C and CSC/Pamplona continued to conduct business and legal due diligence.\n1687: On\n1688: September 18, 2013, CSC/Pamplona submitted a proposal for an all-cash purchase price of $20.75 per share, reiterating that Pamplona was committed to provide 100% of the capital\n1689: required to consummate the transaction and requesting that CSC/Pamplona and Mac-Gray agree to engage in exclusive negotiations for two weeks. Also on September 18, 2013, Party A reiterated in a\n1690: telephone call to a representative of BofA Merrill Lynch that its previous indication of interest with an all-cash purchase price of $18.00 to $19.00 per share was its best and final offer. On\n1691: September 18, 2013, Party B also submitted a revised indication of interest with a purchase price that Party B valued at $21.50 per share, including $19.00 of cash to be paid at closing\n1692: and the remaining per share price to be paid in the form of options to be issued to Mac-Gray stockholders, representing 10% of the new equity with a strike price equal to Party B's initial cost basis.\n1693: These options, which we refer to as the Party B options, would vest on the achievement of the base case management projections (\n1694: i.e.\n1695: , over five years),\n1696: excluding any future acquisitions. Party B's proposal stated that it valued the Party B options at $2.50 of incremental value per share. Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or\n1697: reiterate its prior indication of interest of an all-cash purchase price of $16.00 to $17.00 per share nor did it specify any reasons in connection therewith.\n1698: On\n1699: September 19, 2013, the Special Committee held an in-person meeting. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Shea, as well as\n1700: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter, were also present for all or portions of the meeting. At this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch updated the Special Committee as to the current\n1701: status of the revised indications of interest submitted by Party A, Party B and CSC/Pamplona and reported that Party C did not submit a revised indication of interest or reiterate its prior indication\n1702: of interest. BofA Merrill Lynch also reported to the Special Committee on Mr. MacDonald's general support for continuing with the process and his view that Mac-Gray should continue to seek a\n1703: higher value for stockholders. Mr. Shea again reviewed the Projections with the Special Committee and BofA Merrill Lynch. The Special Committee again discussed the Projections, including the\n1704: risks relating to Mac-Gray's ability to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry\n1705: facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate markets historically dominated by smaller\n1706: 36\n1707: Table of Contents\n1708: local\n1709: and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could reduce Mac-Gray's stock price). The Special\n1710: Committee also again discussed the potential opportunities if Mac-Gray were able to execute its strategic plan as an independent, stand-alone company (including the ability to improve profitability\n1711: and EBITDA margins by increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from\n1712: coin-operated technology to card-based technology). The Special Committee also discussed the risks involved if Mac-Gray did not execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with\n1713: increasing the Company's stock price without a significant or transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring. The Special Committee then considered the Projections in light\n1714: of the revised indications of interest. Representatives from Goodwin Procter again reviewed the Special Committee's fiduciary duties in the context of a sale of the company. The Special Committee then\n1715: discussed each of the three revised proposals, including their advantages and disadvantages. The Special Committee discussed the risks and complexities of the Party B options, including their\n1716: contingent and deferred nature and the uncertainties associated with how Mac-Gray's performance would be measured, especially in light of the fact that current Mac-Gray management would not be in\n1717: control of the surviving corporation to monitor or drive the growth necessary to trigger payment under the Party B options.\n1718: The\n1719: representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch were then excused from the meeting and the Special Committee continued its discussion. The Special Committee noted that the indication of\n1720: interest submitted by Party B, including the Party B options, was on its face higher than the CSC/Pamplona proposal but further discussed the risks and complexities involved with measuring and\n1721: achieving the performance required to vest the Party B options. The Special Committee also noted that Party B would likely be financing the transaction with a combination of equity from affiliated\n1722: funds and third party debt capital, which would involve more risk than the Pamplona-funded CSC/Pamplona proposal, which did not require any third party financing. Following a discussion, the Special\n1723: Committee concluded that the revised indication of interest submitted by CSC/Pamplona had the potential of delivering the most value to stockholders, provided greater certainty and involved less\n1724: potential risk than the other indications. However, the Special Committee also concluded that, primarily in view of potential synergies between Mac-Gray and CSC, CSC/Pamplona should be in a position\n1725: to increase their offer price above $20.75. The Special Committee then authorized the Transaction Committee to inform CSC/Pamplona that the Special Committee would be prepared to move into exclusivity\n1726: at a price of $21.25 per share and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to negotiate the terms of such exclusivity based on guidance provided by the Special Committee. In addition,\n1727: at this meeting Mr. MacDonald indicated that he would be supportive of a price of $21.25 per share.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1728]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 260, \"offset\": 1468, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824954961,"end":1789824955144}}}}
