{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824956907,"sessionID":"ses_f461ef367ffe0p6nB1IHi4bUUU","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e179470017RP0o19yNDyLGD","sessionID":"ses_f461ef367ffe0p6nB1IHi4bUUU","messageID":"msg_0b9e173170019sSQ5K9148odYI","type":"tool","id":"call_00_OrpkeElXyiCNzSu2aYaa8262","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":260,"offset":1728,"path":"/tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt, lines 1728-1987\n1728: On\n1729: September 21, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee called the representative of CSC/Pamplona to propose that Mac-Gray was prepared to move forward with CSC/Pamplona\n1730: on an exclusive basis for two weeks subject to CSC/Pamplona increasing their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share. Later that same day, the representative of CSC/Pamplona called the members of\n1731: the Transaction Committee to confirm that CSC/Pamplona were willing to increase their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share as a last and best offer, provided that Mac-Gray agree to a two week\n1732: period of exclusive negotiations. The representative of CSC/Pamplona also requested that shares of Mac-Gray\n1733: common stock owned beneficially and of record by Mr. MacDonald and his immediate family be subject to a customary voting agreement, which we refer to as the MacDonald voting agreements.\n1734: Between\n1735: September 21, 2013 and September 23, 2013, the parties negotiated the terms of the exclusivity agreement, including an expiration date of October 12, 2013,\n1736: the inclusion of a $15 million \"reverse\" termination fee payable by CSC/Pamplona to Mac-Gray in the event regulatory clearance is not obtained, which we refer to as the CSC reverse termination\n1737: fee, agreements regarding certain post-signing covenants, the fact that there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was\n1738: 37\n1739: Table of Contents\n1740: committing\n1741: to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing, which, together with the all-cash per share purchase price of $21.25, we refer to as the CSC/Pamplona final proposal. The parties also agreed\n1742: that the exclusivity agreement would automatically terminate if CSC or Pamplona adversely changed any of the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n1743: On\n1744: September 23, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. Rothenberg and his counsel to inform Mr. Rothenberg,\n1745: on a limited basis, of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC and Pamplona to provide Moab with the opportunity to resume\n1746: discussions with CSC/Pamplona regarding a possible equity rollover of Moab's shares in the transaction if Moab still had an interest in such a rollover. Mr. Rothenberg indicated he would like\n1747: to discuss the possibility of an equity rollover with CSC/Pamplona and it was agreed that representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch would arrange and facilitate such a discussion.\n1748: Later\n1749: on September 23, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the negotiations from the Transaction Committee and\n1750: Goodwin Procter regarding the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and request for exclusivity. Representatives from Goodwin Procter reviewed the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal with the Special\n1751: Committee, including the $21.25 per share cash consideration, the $15 million CSC reverse termination fee, certain post-signing covenants, the fact that there would be no financing contingency\n1752: and that Pamplona was committing to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing. After discussion, the Special Committee authorized Mac-Gray to enter into an exclusivity agreement providing for\n1753: exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 on the terms included in the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to\n1754: continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona based on guidance provided by the Special Committee at this meeting.\n1755: On\n1756: September 24, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed the exclusivity agreement providing for exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013,\n1757: subject to certain conditions, including that CSC and Pamplona not adversely change or modify the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n1758: On\n1759: September 25, 2013, Goodwin Procter delivered to Kirkland & Ellis LLP, outside legal counsel to CSC/Pamplona, whom we refer to as Kirkland, the initial draft of\n1760: the merger agreement. Between September 25, 2013 and October 7, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Goodwin Procter and Kirkland negotiated the terms\n1761: of the merger agreement (including our ability to respond to unsolicited inquiries following the announcement of the transaction and our right to terminate the merger agreement to accept a superior\n1762: proposal) and the Pamplona commitment letter. The parties discussed various open points in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including the proposal by Mac-Gray that both\n1763: Pamplona and CSC be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including liability for any and all damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing\n1764: covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amount of the termination fee payable by Mac-Gray to CSC under certain circumstances (which we refer to as the Mac-Gray termination fee),\n1765: which Mac-Gray proposed should approximate 2.5% of the total equity value of the transaction. Also during this time, CSC and Pamplona were granted full access to the electronic data site and access to\n1766: certain members of management and conducted confirmatory business and legal due diligence on Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg engaged in various discussions with representatives of Pamplona during this\n1767: time to discuss a possible rollover of Moab shares. As a result of such discussions, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Following such determination,\n1768: BofA Merrill Lynch and the Transaction Committee provided Mr. Rothenberg with the details regarding the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between\n1769: Mac-Gray and CSC/Pamplona.\n1770: 38\n1771: Table of Contents\n1772: Between\n1773: September 24, 2013 and September 27, 2013, Kirkland and outside counsel to Mr. MacDonald negotiated the terms of the MacDonald voting agreements, which were\n1774: entered into by Mr. MacDonald, his wife and one of his trusts on September 27, 2013, and which became effective upon entry into the merger agreement.\n1775: On\n1776: October 1, 2013, representatives of Mac-Gray management, along with representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives from CSC/Pamplona attended a meeting at the\n1777: Boston offices of Goodwin Procter for purposes of conducting confirmatory business due diligence. Representatives of Mac-Gray, Goodwin Procter, BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of CSC/Pamplona\n1778: and Kirkland participated in various telephone conference calls over the subsequent days to finalize business and legal due diligence.\n1779: On\n1780: October 5, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the Pamplona commitment letter which, among other things, proposed that, in addition to CSC, Pamplona\n1781: would be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, but that Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement would be limited to an unspecified\n1782: dollar amount.\n1783: On\n1784: October 7, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee\n1785: and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present for a portion of the meeting. Mr. Daoust reported to the Special Committee that, after\n1786: discussions between Moab and representatives of Pamplona, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Mr. Daoust also provided the Special Committee with\n1787: an overview of the open business points and the progress the parties had made since the September 23\n1788: rd\n1789: Special Committee meeting. Representatives from Goodwin Procter then\n1790: reported on the progress that had been made in diligence and summarized the open issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including to what extent Pamplona would be\n1791: responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and\n1792: the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The Special Committee authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin\n1793: Procter to continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona, and, among other things, provided guidance with respect to the open transaction issues discussed at the meeting.\n1794: Also\n1795: on October 7, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the merger agreement which, among other things, proposed a $15 million Mac-Gray\n1796: termination fee, which represented approximately 4.4% of the total equity value of the transaction, and certain positions on the scope of various post-signing covenants.\n1797: On\n1798: October 8, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter and Kirkland participated in a telephone conference call to discuss the open points in the merger agreement and the\n1799: Pamplona commitment letter, including to what extent Pamplona would also have liability in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, which Kirkland proposed, at the direction of CSC/Pamplona, be\n1800: capped at $50 million. Kirkland and Goodwin Procter also discussed CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and\n1801: the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The parties also discussed potential compromises on the post-signing\n1802: covenants. Goodwin Procter and Kirkland agreed to communicate these positions to their respective clients. Later that day, after various discussions among representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Kirkland,\n1803: Goodwin Procter and the members of the Transaction Committee, and on behalf of the Special Committee, Goodwin Procter sent an e-mail to the representatives of Kirkland that proposed to accept the\n1804: 39\n1805: Table of Contents\n1806: $50 million\n1807: liability cap proposed by Pamplona and proposed a $10.5 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which represented approximately 3.0% of the total equity value of the transaction.\n1808: On\n1809: October 9, 2013, in follow up to the October 8, 2013 negotiations and proposals made by Goodwin Procter, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a\n1810: telephone conference call with a representative from CSC/Pamplona to discuss the open issues in the merger agreement, including certain post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights,\n1811: including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee, but did not reach agreement on these issues.\n1812: Later\n1813: on October 9, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction\n1814: Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present at this meeting. The Transaction Committee reported to the Special Committee on the\n1815: negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held on October 7, 2013. The Transaction Committee reported that the parties had come to an agreement that\n1816: Pamplona's liability for certain damages would be limited to $50 million, but that certain post-signing covenants and the amounts of the termination fees remained open issues. The Special\n1817: Committee discussed various alternatives that could be proposed to CSC/Pamplona on the open issues. Goodwin Procter then reported that Kirkland had requested on behalf of CSC/Pamplona that the\n1818: exclusivity agreement, due to expire at 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013, be extended to allow for continued negotiations of these open issues. After discussion, the Special Committee\n1819: authorized the Transaction Committee to negotiate the open issues within certain parameters and in connection therewith to extend exclusivity to October 15, 2013. The Special Committee also\n1820: determined to reconvene for an update telephone conference call on October 11, 2013.\n1821: On\n1822: October 10, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee and the representative of CSC/Pamplona engaged in various discussions regarding the open issues, including certain\n1823: post-signing covenants and the amounts of the termination fees. While the parties reached a compromise on the post-signing covenants, no agreement was reached on the amount of the Mac-Gray termination\n1824: fee. On October 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to discuss the proposed terms of the transaction and the status of discussions with CSC/Pamplona. By invitation of the\n1825: Special Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of Goodwin Procter were also present. The members of the Transaction Committee reviewed with the Special Committee the negotiations that\n1826: had taken place with representatives from CSC/Pamplona since the October 9\n1827: th\n1828: Special Committee meeting. The Transaction Committee reported that CSC/Pamplona, on the one\n1829: hand, and the Transaction Committee (as authorized by the Special Committee at the October 9\n1830: th\n1831: meeting), on the other hand, had come to an agreement on certain post-signing\n1832: covenants but that the parties had not reached agreement on the amount of the Mac-Gray termination fee. The Special Committee also discussed the fact that the exclusivity agreement would be expiring\n1833: at 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 and that CSC/Pamplona had requested an extension of exclusivity until the end of the day on October 15, 2013. After discussions, the Special\n1834: Committee determined that the members of the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter should communicate to the representative of CSC/Pamplona and representatives at Kirkland,\n1835: respectively, that the Special Committee continued to object to the $15 million Mac-Gray termination fee proposed by CSC/Pamplona and would not be willing to extend exclusivity unless a lower\n1836: amount were agreed to. The Special Committee confirmed that the Transaction Committee and representatives of Mac-Gray were authorized to extend the exclusivity agreement until 11:59 p.m. on ET\n1837: on October 15, 2013 if CSC/Pamplona would agree to a Mac-Gray termination fee of not more than $11 million, which represented approximately 3.2% of the total equity value of the\n1838: transaction.\n1839: Later\n1840: on October 11, 2013, the two members of the Transaction Committee telephoned CSC/Pamplona and communicated the Special Committee's position. Shortly following that\n1841: conversation, the representative of CSC/Pamplona telephoned Mr. Daoust and agreed to the $11 million Mac-Gray\n1842: 40\n1843: Table of Contents\n1844: termination\n1845: fee. Following such agreement, Goodwin Procter delivered Kirkland revised drafts of the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter.\n1846: On\n1847: October 12, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed a letter agreement extending exclusivity until 11:59 p.m. ET on October 15, 2013.\n1848: Between\n1849: October 12, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and Goodwin Procter finalized the remaining unresolved issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment\n1850: letter.\n1851: Between\n1852: October 8, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and counsel for Moab negotiated the terms of a voting agreement for Moab.\n1853: On\n1854: October 14, 2013, the Special Committee held a meeting via teleconference to discuss the final terms of the transaction and the proposed definitive merger agreement and related\n1855: documents. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. The members\n1856: of the Transaction Committee reported to the Special Committee on the negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held on October 11, 2013, and in particular\n1857: that the Transaction Committee and CSC had come to an agreement on the remaining open business points, including the post-signing covenants and the termination fees. Goodwin Procter again reviewed\n1858: with the Special Committee its fiduciary duties and provided a summary of the terms of the proposed merger agreement and the Pamplona capital commitment letter, copies of which were provided to the\n1859: directors prior to the meeting. Also at this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee its financial analysis of the consideration and delivered to the Special Committee an oral\n1860: opinion, which was confirmed by delivery of a written opinion dated, October 14, 2013, to the effect that, as of that date and based on and subject to the assumptions and limitations described\n1861: in its opinion, the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders. Following these presentations, the Special Committee\n1862: went into an executive session that excluded BofA Merrill Lynch and, after discussion, the Special Committee unanimously determined to recommend to the Board that the merger agreement and the merger\n1863: were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's stockholders and that the Board approve the merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger agreement, adopt the merger\n1864: agreement and recommend that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the merger agreement. The Special Committee meeting then adjourned.\n1865: Following\n1866: the adjournment of the Special Committee meeting, a meeting of the Mac-Gray Board of Directors convened. All members of the Board participated. Representatives from Goodwin\n1867: Procter led a discussion on the terms of the merger agreement and advised the Board regarding the likely timing for execution of a definitive agreement and public announcement of the transaction and a\n1868: projected closing timetable should the Board approve the merger. After discussion, the Board (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) unanimously determined that the merger agreement and the\n1869: merger were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's stockholders, approved the merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger agreement, adopted the merger\n1870: agreement and recommended that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the merger agreement.\n1871: Later\n1872: in the day, on October 14, 2013, the merger agreement was executed, the Pamplona commitment letter was delivered, Moab and Parent executed the Moab voting agreement and the\n1873: Mac-Donald voting agreements became effective.\n1874: On\n1875: October 15, 2013, before the U.S. stock markets opened, Mac-Gray and CSC jointly announced the execution of the merger agreement.\n1876: 41\n1877: Table of Contents\n1878: Reasons for the Merger and Recommendation of our Board of Directors\n1879: Reasons for the Merger\n1880: In the course of reaching its decision to approve the merger and the merger agreement, the Special Committee and the full Board of\n1881: Directors held numerous meetings and consulted with the Transaction Committee, our senior management, Goodwin Procter and BofA Merrill Lynch, reviewed a significant amount of information and\n1882: considered a number of factors, including the following factors:\n1883: •\n1884: information concerning our business, financial performance (both past and prospective) and our financial condition,\n1885: results of operations (both past and prospective), business and strategic objectives, as well as the risks and benefits of accomplishing those objectives;\n1886: •\n1887: opportunities if Mac-Gray were to remain independent, including the ability to improve profitability and EBITDA margins by\n1888: increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to\n1889: card-based technology;\n1890: •\n1891: risks associated with executing and achieving Mac-Gray's short and long-term business and financial plans, including\n1892: Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate\n1893: markets historically dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could\n1894: reduce Mac-Gray's stock price and the risks involved if Mac-Gray was not able to execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with increasing stock price without a significant or\n1895: transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring;\n1896: •\n1897: the possible alternatives to the merger (including the possibility of continuing to operate as an independent entity and a\n1898: stock repurchase, and the perceived risks thereof), the range of possible benefits to our stockholders of those alternatives and the timing and the likelihood of accomplishing the goal of any of such\n1899: alternatives, and the Special Committee's assessment that the merger with CSC presented a superior opportunity to such alternatives for our stockholders;\n1900: •\n1901: the results of discussions with third parties, including Party A, Party B, Party C and other potential bidders, relating\n1902: to a possible business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1903: •\n1904: the process undertaken by our Board of Directors and the Special Committee, assisted by its legal and financial advisors,\n1905: in connection with pursuing a strategic transaction and the terms and conditions of the proposed merger, in each case in light of the current market dynamics in the industry;\n1906: •\n1907: current financial market conditions and historical market prices, volatility and trading information with respect to\n1908: Mac-Gray common stock;\n1909: •\n1910: the potential for obtaining a superior offer from an alternative purchaser in light of the other potential purchasers\n1911: previously identified and contacted by BofA Merrill Lynch at the instruction of the Special Committee, including Party A, Party B and Party C, and the risk of losing the proposed transaction with\n1912: CSC/Pamplona; and\n1913: •\n1914: the terms of the merger agreement, including the parties' representations, warranties and covenants, the certainty of\n1915: financing, the conditions to parties' respective obligations and the termination rights of the parties.\n1916: 42\n1917: Table of Contents\n1918: In\n1919: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following positive\n1920: factors:\n1921: •\n1922: the value of the consideration to be received by our stockholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1923: •\n1924: the fact that the $21.25 per share to be paid as the consideration in the merger represents a premium of approximately 36%\n1925: over the high of $15.61 per share for the 52-week period ending October 11, 2013 and a premium of approximately 44% over the $14.72 closing trading price for the shares of our common stock on\n1926: the NYSE on October 11, 2013, the last trading day prior to the date our Board of Directors approved the merger agreement;\n1927: •\n1928: the multiple of our EBITDA and revenue represented by the $21.25 per share purchase price relative to EBITDA and revenue\n1929: multiples represented by the consideration paid in comparable precedent transactions;\n1930: •\n1931: the opinion of BofA Merrill Lynch, dated October 14, 2013, to the Special Committee as to the fairness, from a\n1932: financial point of view and as of the date of the opinion, of the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock, as more fully described below in the section entitled \"Opinion of\n1933: Mac-Gray's Financial Advisor\";\n1934: •\n1935: the likelihood that the proposed acquisition would be consummated, in light of the experience, reputation and financial\n1936: capabilities of CSC/Pamplona;\n1937: •\n1938: the Pamplona commitment letter, pursuant to which Pamplona has committed to fund up to $594 million to the Buyer\n1939: Entities to fund the payment of the merger consideration and other amounts payable pursuant to the merger agreement or to satisfy any damages (up to $50 million) awarded to Mac-Gray in certain\n1940: circumstances involving litigation brought pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1941: •\n1942: CSC and Merger Subsidiary representations to us in the merger agreement that they will have adequate capital resources to\n1943: pay the merger consideration and other amounts to consummate the merger, taking into account the proceeds from the Pamplona commitment letter;\n1944: •\n1945: the form and timing of merger consideration, consisting solely of cash at closing, which provides certainty of value to\n1946: our stockholders;\n1947: •\n1948: the process through which Mac-Gray, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch, at the instruction of the Special\n1949: Committee, engaged in or sought to engage in discussions with other companies believed to be the most likely candidates to pursue a business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1950: •\n1951: the belief of the Special Committee and the Mac-Gray Board of Directors that, after extensive negotiations with\n1952: CSC/Pamplona and their representatives, we have obtained the highest price per share that CSC/Pamplona are willing to pay and the highest price obtainable on the date of signing of the merger\n1953: agreement;\n1954: •\n1955: the merger agreement, subject to the limitations and requirements contained in the agreement, provides our Board of\n1956: Directors (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) with flexibility to furnish information to and conduct negotiations with third parties in certain circumstances and, upon payment to CSC of\n1957: the Mac-Gray termination fee $11 million (which was heavily negotiated and which our Board of Directors believes is reasonable under the circumstances) to terminate the merger agreement, to\n1958: accept a superior offer;\n1959: •\n1960: the fact that the confidentiality and standstill agreements entered into with potential bidders contain sunset provisions\n1961: for the standstill restrictions, which permits these potential bidders to submit unsolicited offers to acquire Mac-Gray;\n1962: 43\n1963: Table of Contents\n1964: •\n1965: the other terms and conditions of the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including among other things\n1966: the size of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the size of the CSC reverse termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the scope\n1967: of the post-signing covenants; the limited number and nature of the conditions to the Buyer Entities' obligation to complete the merger, including (but not limited to) the absence of a financing\n1968: condition and the adequacy of CSC's capital resources to pay the merger consideration; the fact that Pamplona will be, subject to certain conditions and limitations, responsible to fund the merger\n1969: consideration to cause the closing or to pay certain damages if the closing does not occur; and the definition of \"material adverse effect\" (including the exceptions for what constitutes a material\n1970: adverse effect for purposes of the merger agreement); and\n1971: •\n1972: the voting agreements terminate in the event that we terminate the merger agreement which permits those stockholders to\n1973: support a transaction involving a superior offer.\n1974: In\n1975: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following negative\n1976: factors:\n1977: •\n1978: the potential loss of customer, supplier or other commercial relationships of Mac-Gray as a result of the merger and/or\n1979: the announcement of the merger or other potential disruption to customer, supplier, vendor or other commercial relationships important to us as a result of the merger and/or the announcement of the\n1980: merger;\n1981: •\n1982: the possibility that the merger will not be consummated and the potential negative effect of the public announcement of\n1983: the merger on our sales, operating results and stock price and our ability to retain employees;\n1984: •\n1985: the fact that, after the merger, our stockholders would not participate in any future growth potential or benefit from any\n1986: future increase in our value;\n1987: •\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1988]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt, lines 1728-1987\n1728: On\n1729: September 21, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee called the representative of CSC/Pamplona to propose that Mac-Gray was prepared to move forward with CSC/Pamplona\n1730: on an exclusive basis for two weeks subject to CSC/Pamplona increasing their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share. Later that same day, the representative of CSC/Pamplona called the members of\n1731: the Transaction Committee to confirm that CSC/Pamplona were willing to increase their all-cash purchase price to $21.25 per share as a last and best offer, provided that Mac-Gray agree to a two week\n1732: period of exclusive negotiations. The representative of CSC/Pamplona also requested that shares of Mac-Gray\n1733: common stock owned beneficially and of record by Mr. MacDonald and his immediate family be subject to a customary voting agreement, which we refer to as the MacDonald voting agreements.\n1734: Between\n1735: September 21, 2013 and September 23, 2013, the parties negotiated the terms of the exclusivity agreement, including an expiration date of October 12, 2013,\n1736: the inclusion of a $15 million \"reverse\" termination fee payable by CSC/Pamplona to Mac-Gray in the event regulatory clearance is not obtained, which we refer to as the CSC reverse termination\n1737: fee, agreements regarding certain post-signing covenants, the fact that there would be no financing contingency and that Pamplona was\n1738: 37\n1739: Table of Contents\n1740: committing\n1741: to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing, which, together with the all-cash per share purchase price of $21.25, we refer to as the CSC/Pamplona final proposal. The parties also agreed\n1742: that the exclusivity agreement would automatically terminate if CSC or Pamplona adversely changed any of the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n1743: On\n1744: September 23, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter participated in a telephone conference call with Mr. Rothenberg and his counsel to inform Mr. Rothenberg,\n1745: on a limited basis, of the status of the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between Mac-Gray and CSC and Pamplona to provide Moab with the opportunity to resume\n1746: discussions with CSC/Pamplona regarding a possible equity rollover of Moab's shares in the transaction if Moab still had an interest in such a rollover. Mr. Rothenberg indicated he would like\n1747: to discuss the possibility of an equity rollover with CSC/Pamplona and it was agreed that representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch would arrange and facilitate such a discussion.\n1748: Later\n1749: on September 23, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the negotiations from the Transaction Committee and\n1750: Goodwin Procter regarding the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and request for exclusivity. Representatives from Goodwin Procter reviewed the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal with the Special\n1751: Committee, including the $21.25 per share cash consideration, the $15 million CSC reverse termination fee, certain post-signing covenants, the fact that there would be no financing contingency\n1752: and that Pamplona was committing to fund 100% of the capital needed for closing. After discussion, the Special Committee authorized Mac-Gray to enter into an exclusivity agreement providing for\n1753: exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 on the terms included in the CSC/Pamplona final proposal and authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin Procter to\n1754: continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona based on guidance provided by the Special Committee at this meeting.\n1755: On\n1756: September 24, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed the exclusivity agreement providing for exclusive negotiations until 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013,\n1757: subject to certain conditions, including that CSC and Pamplona not adversely change or modify the terms of the CSC/Pamplona final proposal.\n1758: On\n1759: September 25, 2013, Goodwin Procter delivered to Kirkland & Ellis LLP, outside legal counsel to CSC/Pamplona, whom we refer to as Kirkland, the initial draft of\n1760: the merger agreement. Between September 25, 2013 and October 7, 2013, the Transaction Committee and representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Goodwin Procter and Kirkland negotiated the terms\n1761: of the merger agreement (including our ability to respond to unsolicited inquiries following the announcement of the transaction and our right to terminate the merger agreement to accept a superior\n1762: proposal) and the Pamplona commitment letter. The parties discussed various open points in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including the proposal by Mac-Gray that both\n1763: Pamplona and CSC be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including liability for any and all damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing\n1764: covenants and the parties' termination rights, including the amount of the termination fee payable by Mac-Gray to CSC under certain circumstances (which we refer to as the Mac-Gray termination fee),\n1765: which Mac-Gray proposed should approximate 2.5% of the total equity value of the transaction. Also during this time, CSC and Pamplona were granted full access to the electronic data site and access to\n1766: certain members of management and conducted confirmatory business and legal due diligence on Mac-Gray. Mr. Rothenberg engaged in various discussions with representatives of Pamplona during this\n1767: time to discuss a possible rollover of Moab shares. As a result of such discussions, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Following such determination,\n1768: BofA Merrill Lynch and the Transaction Committee provided Mr. Rothenberg with the details regarding the Special Committee's review of strategic alternatives and the negotiations between\n1769: Mac-Gray and CSC/Pamplona.\n1770: 38\n1771: Table of Contents\n1772: Between\n1773: September 24, 2013 and September 27, 2013, Kirkland and outside counsel to Mr. MacDonald negotiated the terms of the MacDonald voting agreements, which were\n1774: entered into by Mr. MacDonald, his wife and one of his trusts on September 27, 2013, and which became effective upon entry into the merger agreement.\n1775: On\n1776: October 1, 2013, representatives of Mac-Gray management, along with representatives from BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives from CSC/Pamplona attended a meeting at the\n1777: Boston offices of Goodwin Procter for purposes of conducting confirmatory business due diligence. Representatives of Mac-Gray, Goodwin Procter, BofA Merrill Lynch and representatives of CSC/Pamplona\n1778: and Kirkland participated in various telephone conference calls over the subsequent days to finalize business and legal due diligence.\n1779: On\n1780: October 5, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the Pamplona commitment letter which, among other things, proposed that, in addition to CSC, Pamplona\n1781: would be responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, but that Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement would be limited to an unspecified\n1782: dollar amount.\n1783: On\n1784: October 7, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction Committee\n1785: and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present for a portion of the meeting. Mr. Daoust reported to the Special Committee that, after\n1786: discussions between Moab and representatives of Pamplona, it was determined that Moab would not rollover any Moab shares in the transaction. Mr. Daoust also provided the Special Committee with\n1787: an overview of the open business points and the progress the parties had made since the September 23\n1788: rd\n1789: Special Committee meeting. Representatives from Goodwin Procter then\n1790: reported on the progress that had been made in diligence and summarized the open issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including to what extent Pamplona would be\n1791: responsible in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, including CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and\n1792: the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The Special Committee authorized the Transaction Committee and Goodwin\n1793: Procter to continue negotiations with CSC/Pamplona, and, among other things, provided guidance with respect to the open transaction issues discussed at the meeting.\n1794: Also\n1795: on October 7, 2013, Kirkland delivered to Goodwin Procter a revised draft of the merger agreement which, among other things, proposed a $15 million Mac-Gray\n1796: termination fee, which represented approximately 4.4% of the total equity value of the transaction, and certain positions on the scope of various post-signing covenants.\n1797: On\n1798: October 8, 2013, representatives from Goodwin Procter and Kirkland participated in a telephone conference call to discuss the open points in the merger agreement and the\n1799: Pamplona commitment letter, including to what extent Pamplona would also have liability in the event CSC failed to consummate the merger, which Kirkland proposed, at the direction of CSC/Pamplona, be\n1800: capped at $50 million. Kirkland and Goodwin Procter also discussed CSC's and Pamplona's liability for damages resulting from a breach of the merger agreement, certain post-signing covenants and\n1801: the parties' termination rights, including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee. The parties also discussed potential compromises on the post-signing\n1802: covenants. Goodwin Procter and Kirkland agreed to communicate these positions to their respective clients. Later that day, after various discussions among representatives from CSC/Pamplona, Kirkland,\n1803: Goodwin Procter and the members of the Transaction Committee, and on behalf of the Special Committee, Goodwin Procter sent an e-mail to the representatives of Kirkland that proposed to accept the\n1804: 39\n1805: Table of Contents\n1806: $50 million\n1807: liability cap proposed by Pamplona and proposed a $10.5 million Mac-Gray termination fee, which represented approximately 3.0% of the total equity value of the transaction.\n1808: On\n1809: October 9, 2013, in follow up to the October 8, 2013 negotiations and proposals made by Goodwin Procter, the members of the Transaction Committee participated in a\n1810: telephone conference call with a representative from CSC/Pamplona to discuss the open issues in the merger agreement, including certain post-signing covenants and the parties' termination rights,\n1811: including the amounts of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the CSC reverse termination fee, but did not reach agreement on these issues.\n1812: Later\n1813: on October 9, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting, the purpose of which was to receive an update on the transaction negotiations from the Transaction\n1814: Committee and Goodwin Procter. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. MacDonald was also present at this meeting. The Transaction Committee reported to the Special Committee on the\n1815: negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held on October 7, 2013. The Transaction Committee reported that the parties had come to an agreement that\n1816: Pamplona's liability for certain damages would be limited to $50 million, but that certain post-signing covenants and the amounts of the termination fees remained open issues. The Special\n1817: Committee discussed various alternatives that could be proposed to CSC/Pamplona on the open issues. Goodwin Procter then reported that Kirkland had requested on behalf of CSC/Pamplona that the\n1818: exclusivity agreement, due to expire at 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013, be extended to allow for continued negotiations of these open issues. After discussion, the Special Committee\n1819: authorized the Transaction Committee to negotiate the open issues within certain parameters and in connection therewith to extend exclusivity to October 15, 2013. The Special Committee also\n1820: determined to reconvene for an update telephone conference call on October 11, 2013.\n1821: On\n1822: October 10, 2013, the members of the Transaction Committee and the representative of CSC/Pamplona engaged in various discussions regarding the open issues, including certain\n1823: post-signing covenants and the amounts of the termination fees. While the parties reached a compromise on the post-signing covenants, no agreement was reached on the amount of the Mac-Gray termination\n1824: fee. On October 11, 2013, the Special Committee held a telephonic meeting to discuss the proposed terms of the transaction and the status of discussions with CSC/Pamplona. By invitation of the\n1825: Special Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of Goodwin Procter were also present. The members of the Transaction Committee reviewed with the Special Committee the negotiations that\n1826: had taken place with representatives from CSC/Pamplona since the October 9\n1827: th\n1828: Special Committee meeting. The Transaction Committee reported that CSC/Pamplona, on the one\n1829: hand, and the Transaction Committee (as authorized by the Special Committee at the October 9\n1830: th\n1831: meeting), on the other hand, had come to an agreement on certain post-signing\n1832: covenants but that the parties had not reached agreement on the amount of the Mac-Gray termination fee. The Special Committee also discussed the fact that the exclusivity agreement would be expiring\n1833: at 5 p.m. ET on October 12, 2013 and that CSC/Pamplona had requested an extension of exclusivity until the end of the day on October 15, 2013. After discussions, the Special\n1834: Committee determined that the members of the Transaction Committee and representatives of Goodwin Procter should communicate to the representative of CSC/Pamplona and representatives at Kirkland,\n1835: respectively, that the Special Committee continued to object to the $15 million Mac-Gray termination fee proposed by CSC/Pamplona and would not be willing to extend exclusivity unless a lower\n1836: amount were agreed to. The Special Committee confirmed that the Transaction Committee and representatives of Mac-Gray were authorized to extend the exclusivity agreement until 11:59 p.m. on ET\n1837: on October 15, 2013 if CSC/Pamplona would agree to a Mac-Gray termination fee of not more than $11 million, which represented approximately 3.2% of the total equity value of the\n1838: transaction.\n1839: Later\n1840: on October 11, 2013, the two members of the Transaction Committee telephoned CSC/Pamplona and communicated the Special Committee's position. Shortly following that\n1841: conversation, the representative of CSC/Pamplona telephoned Mr. Daoust and agreed to the $11 million Mac-Gray\n1842: 40\n1843: Table of Contents\n1844: termination\n1845: fee. Following such agreement, Goodwin Procter delivered Kirkland revised drafts of the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter.\n1846: On\n1847: October 12, 2013, CSC, Pamplona and Mac-Gray executed a letter agreement extending exclusivity until 11:59 p.m. ET on October 15, 2013.\n1848: Between\n1849: October 12, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and Goodwin Procter finalized the remaining unresolved issues in the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment\n1850: letter.\n1851: Between\n1852: October 8, 2013 and October 14, 2013, Kirkland and counsel for Moab negotiated the terms of a voting agreement for Moab.\n1853: On\n1854: October 14, 2013, the Special Committee held a meeting via teleconference to discuss the final terms of the transaction and the proposed definitive merger agreement and related\n1855: documents. By invitation of the Special Committee, Mr. Rothenberg and representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter were also present for all or portions of the meeting. The members\n1856: of the Transaction Committee reported to the Special Committee on the negotiations that had taken place since the meeting of the Special Committee held on October 11, 2013, and in particular\n1857: that the Transaction Committee and CSC had come to an agreement on the remaining open business points, including the post-signing covenants and the termination fees. Goodwin Procter again reviewed\n1858: with the Special Committee its fiduciary duties and provided a summary of the terms of the proposed merger agreement and the Pamplona capital commitment letter, copies of which were provided to the\n1859: directors prior to the meeting. Also at this meeting, BofA Merrill Lynch reviewed with the Special Committee its financial analysis of the consideration and delivered to the Special Committee an oral\n1860: opinion, which was confirmed by delivery of a written opinion dated, October 14, 2013, to the effect that, as of that date and based on and subject to the assumptions and limitations described\n1861: in its opinion, the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders. Following these presentations, the Special Committee\n1862: went into an executive session that excluded BofA Merrill Lynch and, after discussion, the Special Committee unanimously determined to recommend to the Board that the merger agreement and the merger\n1863: were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's stockholders and that the Board approve the merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger agreement, adopt the merger\n1864: agreement and recommend that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the merger agreement. The Special Committee meeting then adjourned.\n1865: Following\n1866: the adjournment of the Special Committee meeting, a meeting of the Mac-Gray Board of Directors convened. All members of the Board participated. Representatives from Goodwin\n1867: Procter led a discussion on the terms of the merger agreement and advised the Board regarding the likely timing for execution of a definitive agreement and public announcement of the transaction and a\n1868: projected closing timetable should the Board approve the merger. After discussion, the Board (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) unanimously determined that the merger agreement and the\n1869: merger were advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray's stockholders, approved the merger on the terms and subject to the conditions set forth in the merger agreement, adopted the merger\n1870: agreement and recommended that the Mac-Gray stockholders adopt and approve the merger agreement.\n1871: Later\n1872: in the day, on October 14, 2013, the merger agreement was executed, the Pamplona commitment letter was delivered, Moab and Parent executed the Moab voting agreement and the\n1873: Mac-Donald voting agreements became effective.\n1874: On\n1875: October 15, 2013, before the U.S. stock markets opened, Mac-Gray and CSC jointly announced the execution of the merger agreement.\n1876: 41\n1877: Table of Contents\n1878: Reasons for the Merger and Recommendation of our Board of Directors\n1879: Reasons for the Merger\n1880: In the course of reaching its decision to approve the merger and the merger agreement, the Special Committee and the full Board of\n1881: Directors held numerous meetings and consulted with the Transaction Committee, our senior management, Goodwin Procter and BofA Merrill Lynch, reviewed a significant amount of information and\n1882: considered a number of factors, including the following factors:\n1883: •\n1884: information concerning our business, financial performance (both past and prospective) and our financial condition,\n1885: results of operations (both past and prospective), business and strategic objectives, as well as the risks and benefits of accomplishing those objectives;\n1886: •\n1887: opportunities if Mac-Gray were to remain independent, including the ability to improve profitability and EBITDA margins by\n1888: increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to\n1889: card-based technology;\n1890: •\n1891: risks associated with executing and achieving Mac-Gray's short and long-term business and financial plans, including\n1892: Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate\n1893: markets historically dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could\n1894: reduce Mac-Gray's stock price and the risks involved if Mac-Gray was not able to execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with increasing stock price without a significant or\n1895: transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring;\n1896: •\n1897: the possible alternatives to the merger (including the possibility of continuing to operate as an independent entity and a\n1898: stock repurchase, and the perceived risks thereof), the range of possible benefits to our stockholders of those alternatives and the timing and the likelihood of accomplishing the goal of any of such\n1899: alternatives, and the Special Committee's assessment that the merger with CSC presented a superior opportunity to such alternatives for our stockholders;\n1900: •\n1901: the results of discussions with third parties, including Party A, Party B, Party C and other potential bidders, relating\n1902: to a possible business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1903: •\n1904: the process undertaken by our Board of Directors and the Special Committee, assisted by its legal and financial advisors,\n1905: in connection with pursuing a strategic transaction and the terms and conditions of the proposed merger, in each case in light of the current market dynamics in the industry;\n1906: •\n1907: current financial market conditions and historical market prices, volatility and trading information with respect to\n1908: Mac-Gray common stock;\n1909: •\n1910: the potential for obtaining a superior offer from an alternative purchaser in light of the other potential purchasers\n1911: previously identified and contacted by BofA Merrill Lynch at the instruction of the Special Committee, including Party A, Party B and Party C, and the risk of losing the proposed transaction with\n1912: CSC/Pamplona; and\n1913: •\n1914: the terms of the merger agreement, including the parties' representations, warranties and covenants, the certainty of\n1915: financing, the conditions to parties' respective obligations and the termination rights of the parties.\n1916: 42\n1917: Table of Contents\n1918: In\n1919: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following positive\n1920: factors:\n1921: •\n1922: the value of the consideration to be received by our stockholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1923: •\n1924: the fact that the $21.25 per share to be paid as the consideration in the merger represents a premium of approximately 36%\n1925: over the high of $15.61 per share for the 52-week period ending October 11, 2013 and a premium of approximately 44% over the $14.72 closing trading price for the shares of our common stock on\n1926: the NYSE on October 11, 2013, the last trading day prior to the date our Board of Directors approved the merger agreement;\n1927: •\n1928: the multiple of our EBITDA and revenue represented by the $21.25 per share purchase price relative to EBITDA and revenue\n1929: multiples represented by the consideration paid in comparable precedent transactions;\n1930: •\n1931: the opinion of BofA Merrill Lynch, dated October 14, 2013, to the Special Committee as to the fairness, from a\n1932: financial point of view and as of the date of the opinion, of the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock, as more fully described below in the section entitled \"Opinion of\n1933: Mac-Gray's Financial Advisor\";\n1934: •\n1935: the likelihood that the proposed acquisition would be consummated, in light of the experience, reputation and financial\n1936: capabilities of CSC/Pamplona;\n1937: •\n1938: the Pamplona commitment letter, pursuant to which Pamplona has committed to fund up to $594 million to the Buyer\n1939: Entities to fund the payment of the merger consideration and other amounts payable pursuant to the merger agreement or to satisfy any damages (up to $50 million) awarded to Mac-Gray in certain\n1940: circumstances involving litigation brought pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1941: •\n1942: CSC and Merger Subsidiary representations to us in the merger agreement that they will have adequate capital resources to\n1943: pay the merger consideration and other amounts to consummate the merger, taking into account the proceeds from the Pamplona commitment letter;\n1944: •\n1945: the form and timing of merger consideration, consisting solely of cash at closing, which provides certainty of value to\n1946: our stockholders;\n1947: •\n1948: the process through which Mac-Gray, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch, at the instruction of the Special\n1949: Committee, engaged in or sought to engage in discussions with other companies believed to be the most likely candidates to pursue a business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1950: •\n1951: the belief of the Special Committee and the Mac-Gray Board of Directors that, after extensive negotiations with\n1952: CSC/Pamplona and their representatives, we have obtained the highest price per share that CSC/Pamplona are willing to pay and the highest price obtainable on the date of signing of the merger\n1953: agreement;\n1954: •\n1955: the merger agreement, subject to the limitations and requirements contained in the agreement, provides our Board of\n1956: Directors (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) with flexibility to furnish information to and conduct negotiations with third parties in certain circumstances and, upon payment to CSC of\n1957: the Mac-Gray termination fee $11 million (which was heavily negotiated and which our Board of Directors believes is reasonable under the circumstances) to terminate the merger agreement, to\n1958: accept a superior offer;\n1959: •\n1960: the fact that the confidentiality and standstill agreements entered into with potential bidders contain sunset provisions\n1961: for the standstill restrictions, which permits these potential bidders to submit unsolicited offers to acquire Mac-Gray;\n1962: 43\n1963: Table of Contents\n1964: •\n1965: the other terms and conditions of the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including among other things\n1966: the size of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the size of the CSC reverse termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the scope\n1967: of the post-signing covenants; the limited number and nature of the conditions to the Buyer Entities' obligation to complete the merger, including (but not limited to) the absence of a financing\n1968: condition and the adequacy of CSC's capital resources to pay the merger consideration; the fact that Pamplona will be, subject to certain conditions and limitations, responsible to fund the merger\n1969: consideration to cause the closing or to pay certain damages if the closing does not occur; and the definition of \"material adverse effect\" (including the exceptions for what constitutes a material\n1970: adverse effect for purposes of the merger agreement); and\n1971: •\n1972: the voting agreements terminate in the event that we terminate the merger agreement which permits those stockholders to\n1973: support a transaction involving a superior offer.\n1974: In\n1975: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following negative\n1976: factors:\n1977: •\n1978: the potential loss of customer, supplier or other commercial relationships of Mac-Gray as a result of the merger and/or\n1979: the announcement of the merger or other potential disruption to customer, supplier, vendor or other commercial relationships important to us as a result of the merger and/or the announcement of the\n1980: merger;\n1981: •\n1982: the possibility that the merger will not be consummated and the potential negative effect of the public announcement of\n1983: the merger on our sales, operating results and stock price and our ability to retain employees;\n1984: •\n1985: the fact that, after the merger, our stockholders would not participate in any future growth potential or benefit from any\n1986: future increase in our value;\n1987: •\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1988]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 260, \"offset\": 1728, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/macgray_text.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824956743,"end":1789824956907}}}}
